---
/**
 * Career, English. The "portfolio" of the analogy in
 * information-architecture.md section 1, with POSTS instead of clients: a
 * portfolio per client is exactly what the privacy boundary forbids.
 *
 * Three sections with English anchors, and two page-level blocks that sit at
 * the same level as them: the one-line intro above, and the CTA below. The CTA
 * closes the PAGE and never the last section (section 5.5), which is why this
 * is the only page where that distinction can be got wrong.
 *
 * It does not repeat a single bullet of /what-i-do. Here a post is CONTEXT.
 * And the links run one way only: /career points at competencies, /what-i-do
 * never points back at a post, because a capability that points at a post
 * dates the capability (section 5.3).
 *
 * DENSITY IS A REQUIREMENT HERE, not a preference. The vocational diploma has
 * to weigh less than the engineering degree, and the way that is done is by how
 * much sits at L0: one line against four, with the whole ABS story in its fold
 * (section 4.3).
 *
 * Copy transcribed verbatim from web/content/en/career.md.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import TimelineEntry from '../components/TimelineEntry.astro';
import SectionIndex from '../components/SectionIndex.astro';
import Disclosure from '../components/Disclosure.astro';

const SECTIONS = [
  { id: 'experience', title: 'Experience' },
  { id: 'education', title: 'Education' },
  { id: 'certifications', title: 'Certifications and professional development' },
];
---

<BaseLayout locale="en" current="career" title="Career | Franc Gaya" description="Sixteen years in appliance control electronics, a reconversion from mechanic to electronics engineer, and the education and certifications behind both.">
  <PageHeader>
    <Fragment slot="title">Career</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="lead">Where and when, in order. What I can actually do is on the previous page: <a href="/what-i-do/" class="underline decoration-1 underline-offset-4 hover:text-highlight">What I do</a>.</Fragment>
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-shell px-6 pt-16">
    <SectionIndex label="On this page" items={SECTIONS} />

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-prose space-y-20">
      <!-- A. Experience. Internships stay here rather than moving to Education:
           they are posts, with an organisation and dates, and the section is
           called Experience and not Employment. -->
      <section id="experience" class="scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">Experience</h2>

        <div class="mt-10">
          <TimelineEntry role="Application Reference Engineer (ARE), Product & System Architect" org="E.G.O. Appliance Controls, Lliçà de Vall (Barcelona), Spain" dates="November 2017 to present">
            <p>The ARE role exists because an organization of specialists leaves nobody accountable for the technical whole of a project: every engineer owns one board or one layer, and the gaps between them belong to no one. The ARE is that person. Architecture, requirements, the hardware and software boundary, certification, and the direct technical relationship with the customer.</p>
            <p>I was promoted into it from firmware, on an oven control platform, and I kept the firmware work: the role was added on top rather than instead. Since then I have worked across ovens, range cookers, refrigeration, steam generation, dishwashing, industrial laundry, and professional cooking equipment. What I do in each of those is on <a href="/what-i-do/">What I do</a>.</p>

            <Disclosure id="are-whole" summary="What changes between being the developer and being accountable for the whole">
              <p>The work does not become less technical. It becomes less bounded.</p>
              <p>As a developer, the question is whether the thing I am building is correct. As the person accountable for the whole, correct is necessary and nowhere near sufficient. The question becomes whether it is the right thing at all: whether it can be certified, whether the customer will accept it, whether the engineer who inherits it in four years can maintain it, and whether the decision I am about to take forecloses something we will need later.</p>
              <p>Two consequences that took me a while to learn. The first is that most of my expensive decisions happen before there is anything to test, so the feedback loop is measured in years and the only real defense is writing down why. The second is that the work no longer arrives from one direction: my own projects, older products whose problems resurface, and questions from product management and from R&amp;D management that are not attached to any project of mine. Measuring that load and putting a limit on it turned out to be part of the job, and it is the part nobody warns you about.</p>
            </Disclosure>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <TimelineEntry role="Firmware Engineer (Embedded Software)" org="E.G.O. Appliance Controls, Lliçà de Vall (Barcelona), Spain" dates="November 2012 to October 2017">
            <p>Bare-metal C firmware for home appliance control electronics, from washing machines to ovens: HAL, peripheral drivers, control algorithms, and user interfaces on 8-bit and 16-bit microcontrollers. This is where the platform and safety-concept experience came from, and the ARE role was built on top of it in 2017.</p>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <TimelineEntry role="Firmware & Hardware Engineer, UAV project" org="SingularAircraft (Singular Ideas), Hospitalet de Llobregat, Spain" dates="May to October 2012">
            <p>The only role in my career with hardware and firmware in equal parts, and the only one outside appliances. I built the link between the radio receiver and the aircraft's actuators and sensors, and I did the aircraft's electrical and data wiring myself.</p>

            <Disclosure id="uav-detail" summary="What the aircraft actually needed">
              <p>PPM demodulation of the radio receiver signal, a PID position control loop, electro-hydraulic valve control for the control surfaces, throttle servo control, landing gear deployment, water load and hatches, external digital encoders for position feedback, and remote configuration over RS-232. Plus the PCB design and the complete electrical and data wiring of the aircraft.</p>
              <p>Six months, an 8-bit microcontroller, and an aircraft that had to fly. It was also the first PID loop of my career, which draws a straight line from there to the temperature loops I tune now.</p>
            </Disclosure>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <TimelineEntry role="Firmware Engineer, LED lighting and building automation" org="SAKMA Electrónica Industrial, Hospitalet de Llobregat, Spain" dates="December 2010 to May 2012">
            <p>My first engineering job: firmware for LED luminaires, building automation, and signage devices, all designed and manufactured in-house.</p>

            <Disclosure id="sakma-detail" summary="What I built there, and what stayed with me">
              <p>DALI and DMX lighting controllers, including a twelve-channel DMX controller. A temperature and humidity probe for matrix displays. A light dimmer. A bootloader and firmware updater. A communication link through light itself. And a test station for the production line.</p>
              <p>Three things started there and have followed me ever since: bootloaders, end-of-line test stations, and proprietary communication buses between boards. Fifteen years later I am still doing all three, on considerably larger microcontrollers.</p>
            </Disclosure>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <TimelineEntry role="Engineering internships" org="Aquatec (AGBAR group), FECSA-ENDESA, and EUETIB-UPC, Barcelona" dates="2009 to 2010">
            <p>SCADA screen migration for a drinking water treatment plant (Aquatec). Metering and power upgrade campaigns (FECSA-ENDESA). Mathematics tutoring for first-year engineering students, on an EnginyCat scholarship (EUETIB-UPC), which is the first time I taught anybody anything.</p>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <TimelineEntry role="Automotive and refrigeration mechanic" org="Barcelona" dates="2005 to 2008">
            <p>Cars, then Barcelona's street-cleaning fleet, then refrigeration units for refrigerated trucks. A full reconversion from mechanic to electronics engineer, done in parallel with the degree. <a href="/about/">The long version is in About</a>.</p>
          </TimelineEntry>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- B. Education. The last entry of A (mechanic, 2005 to 2008) sits right
           against the last of B (the vocational diploma, 2004 to 2006), and the
           two explain each other. That is why the link to /about goes above and
           not in the middle of a flat list. -->
      <section id="education" class="scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">Education</h2>

        <div class="mt-10">
          <TimelineEntry role="Ingeniero Técnico Industrial en Electrónica Industrial (Industrial Electronics Engineering)" org="EUETIB, Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya (UPC), Barcelona" dates="2006 to 2010">
            <p>Best academic record of the 2010 graduating class. University's own specialization track in Computing and Telematics. Final project: a complete retro game console, hardware and software, built on a Microchip PIC microcontroller.</p>

            <Disclosure id="final-project" summary="The final project, which was a product and not a program">
              <p>It was a product, not a program, and that is why it still belongs on this page sixteen years later.</p>
              <p>The brief was a retro game console and the deliverable was the machine: schematic and PCB, a PIC microcontroller, an LED matrix display, button controls, and the assembler underneath all of it. Two games run on a small base system that handles the display, the controls, and high-score storage, and the base was deliberately designed so that somebody else could add a third game without touching it.</p>
              <p>Which is, allowing for sixteen years and a few orders of magnitude, the same argument I make today about a base firmware and a parameter table.</p>
              <p>It ended up published twice: the project itself, and a conference paper on introducing retro game console design as a final-year project in the electronics syllabus.</p>
            </Disclosure>
          </TimelineEntry>

          <!-- One line at L0 against four above it. The whole ABS story is in
               the fold, which is what makes this entry weigh less without
               moving it to a different section. -->
          <TimelineEntry role="CFGS in Automotive Technology" org="C.E. Monlau, Barcelona" dates="2004 to 2006">
            <p>Final project: design of an anti-lock braking (ABS) and traction control system.</p>

            <Disclosure id="abs-project" summary="Why a braking system is on an embedded engineer's page">
              <p>Designing an ABS in 2006 was my first closed loop on a physical system. Measure a wheel speed, compare it against what the vehicle is actually doing, act on a valve, and do all of it fast enough that the physics does not get away from you. A sensor, an actuator, a control law, and a hard real-time constraint.</p>
              <p>Twenty years later I tune PID loops on ovens and refrigeration circuits. It is the same problem with a slower plant, and it is why I describe what came next as a change of domain rather than a change of interest.</p>
            </Disclosure>
          </TimelineEntry>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- C. Certifications and professional development. One entry today, and
           what will land here is already identified, so the section is not a
           speculative hole (section 4.3). -->
      <section id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-28">
        <div class="section-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-5 text-h2 text-heading">Certifications and professional development</h2>

        <div class="mt-10">
          <TimelineEntry role="Requirements Management using V-Model (Polarion 22.2)" org="Siemens" dates="November 2023">
            <p>Official course for corporate key users. It is the formal backing for the requirements engineering work on <a href="/what-i-do/#requirements">What I do</a>.</p>
          </TimelineEntry>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
